    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, single episode, moderate, with remission-range score at twelve weeks (F32.1)</content>. The PHQ-9 has fallen from 14 to 4, a 71 percent reduction, meeting the goal of a score below 5. Remission is confirmed if the score stays at 4 or below for a month. No dose increase is needed. Suicide risk remains low. [S]<list><item>Continue sertraline 50 mg daily for at least six months after remission, then review tapering together; do not stop it suddenly.</item><item>PHQ-9 every two to four weeks in the continuation phase, the first at his adult preventive visit in about four weeks.</item><item>Collaborative safety plan reviewed and kept for the continuation phase; 988 and the clinic number confirmed.</item><item>Keep up the walks, court work, and letters.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>Baseline, then at least monthly during acute treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>4, down from 14 at intake (71 percent): response and a remission-range score; the goal of below 5 is met</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Interim PHQ-9 by nurse telephone check</td><td>At least monthly during acute treatment</td><td>Deferred</td><td>The telephone PHQ-9 planned at the six-week visit for about four weeks later was not made, so seven weeks passed without a score, a second deviation from the monthly cadence. No harm resulted: he improved steadily and reports no suicidal thoughts in the interval. Corrective action: continuation-phase PHQ-9s every two to four weeks are booked, the first at his preventive visit in about four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk reassessment (C-SSRS Since Last Visit and PHQ-9 item 9)</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No suicidal thoughts, intent, plan, or behavior since the last visit; PHQ-9 item 9 scored 0. Safety plan reviewed and kept for the continuation phase</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adherence and adverse-effect review</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Misses about one dose every two weeks; no adverse effects</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Tolerability check after a dose change</td><td>1 to 2 weeks after each dose change</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Dose unchanged; no increase needed</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Continuation of sertraline at the remission dose</td><td>At least six months after remission</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Continue 50 mg daily for at least six months, then review tapering together</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight, BMI, and blood pressure</td><td>With each tolerability check</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Weight 83.0 kg, up 1.0 kg since intake; BMI 22.3; blood pressure 114/72 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Mental status examination</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Full, reactive affect; linear thinking; no suicidal ideation or psychotic features</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood Disorder Questionnaire (bipolar screen)</td><td>Repeat if hypomanic features emerge</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Bipolar disorder was not suspected at the psychiatry consultation, and no hypomanic features have appeared</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Drug levels and metabolic laboratory monitoring</td><td>Lithium, valproate, carbamazepine, or second-generation antipsychotic therapy</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Sertraline monotherapy needs no routine laboratory monitoring</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Psychiatry review</td><td>If response stalls, after a failed dose increase, or for new risk</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Consultation completed six weeks ago; none of the criteria for another review are met</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Psychotherapy for relapse prevention (CBT, interpersonal therapy, or mindfulness-based cognitive therapy)</td><td>In continuation for patients at high risk of relapse</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>A single uncomplicated episode in stable remission is not high risk; it stays available if he wants it</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Behavioral activation and sleep routine</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Court work, letters, and evening walks continue; sleeping about seven hours</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Reduce PHQ-9 score from 14 to below 5 by the twelve-week review. Outcome by 2026-09-30: PHQ-9 score 4 [S]</item><item>Take sertraline as prescribed on at least six of seven days each week. Outcome by 2026-09-30: Taken on most days; mild nausea resolved by week three [S]</item><item>Resume at least two valued activities each week, such as court work and letters to friends. Outcome by 2026-08-11: Resumed by the six-week visit [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Adult preventive visit in about four weeks, with a PHQ-9. Depression review every two to four weeks in the continuation phase. Call the clinic or 988 at any time if his mood worsens. Next scheduled visit 2026-10-27: adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

